W: 1. 'Tis of a rich merchant who in London did dwell,
W: He had but one daughter, an uncommon nice gel.
W: Her name it was Dinah, scarce sixteen years old,
W: With a very large fortune in silver and gold.
W:
W: Chorus:
W: Singing tu la lol la rol lal tu rol lal lay.
W:
W: 2. As Dinah was a' walking her garden one day,
W: Her papa he came to her, and thus he did say:
W: "Go dress yourself, Dinah, in gorgeous array,
W: And get you a husband both gallant and gay!"
W:
W: 3. "Oh, papa Oh, papa I've not made up my mind,
W: And to marry just yet, why I don't feel inclined;
W: To you my large fortune I'll gladly give o'er,
W: If you'll let me live single a year or two more."
W:
W: 4. "Go, go, boldest daughter," the parent replicd;
W: "lf you won't consent to be this here young man's bride,
W: I'll give your large fortune to the nearest of kin,
W: And you shan't reap the benefit of one single pin."
W:
W: 5. As Villikins was walking the garden around,
W: He spied his dear Dinah lying dead on the ground;
W: And a cup of cold pizen it lay by her side,
W: With a billet-dou stating 'twas by pizen she died.
W:
W: 6. He kissed her cold corpus a thousand times o'er,
W: He called her his Dinah though she was no more,
W: Then swallowed the pizen like a lover so brave,
W: And Villikins and his Dinah lie both in one grave.
W:
W: Moral:
W: Now all you young maidens take warning by her,
W: Never not by no means disobey your gov'nor,
W: And all you young fellows mind who you clap eyes on,
W: Think of Villikins and Dinah and the cup of cold pizen.
%
